intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) and volumetricmodulated arc therapy (VMAT) can increase the volume of surrounding tissue receiving low-dose radiation [4,5]. Due to the anatomical proximity of the heart, patients treated for primary tumors localized in the left lung are particularly vulnerable to increased cardiac radiation exposure during treatment, and therefore at increased risk for cardiotoxicity. Post-treatment cardiotoxicity contributes to non-cancer mortality in patients treated for lung and bronchus cancer, with Yi et al. demonstrating that radiotherapy (RT) was independently associated with worse cardiovascular-specific survival (HR = 1.18, P < 0.001) and greater deaths from heart disease in patients receiving radiation versus those not [6]. Emerging evidence underscores the critical need to strengthen cardiac protection in patients undergoing lung RT, thus necessitating the accurate prediction and prevention of cardiotoxicity as essential to improving long-term survivorship. Traditionally, thoracic RT planning involves whole-heart contouring as a single organ-at-risk (OAR) on computed tomography (CT) images to simulate, calculate, and minimize radiation exposure as a cost-function of treatment planning. However, emerging evidence indicates that radiation dose to specific cardiac substructures is a more accurate predictor of treatment-associated cardiotoxicity than conventional wholeheart dose [7,8]. Landmark studies have demonstrated that radiation doses to specific cardiac substructures are strongly associated with treatment-related cardiac events following lung RT, while highlighting the limited predictive value of mean whole-heart dose as an OAR metric [9–11]. As the heart functions as a mixed serial-parallel organ, radiation injury to critical substructures, such as the coronary arteries or heart valves, can compromise whole-heart health [12–14]. Current OAR conventions of utilizing whole-heart dose may therefore underestimate cardiac risk by not accounting for heterogeneous dose exposure to substructures that may impart heightened cardiotoxicity risk. Manual contouring of the cardiac substructures is labor-intensive, time-consuming, and subject to inter-observer variability, rendering the task difficult to incorporate into clinical practice. Small substructures, such as the coronary arteries, or those entirely non-visible on CT scans, such as the conduction nodes, are extremely challenging to delineate on simulation CT images, even with contrast enhancement. Patient-to-patient anatomic variability, as well as cardiac and respiratory motion interplay during imaging, obscure the visual quality of substructures and therefore decrease accuracy of contours. Hence, automated deep learning segmentation tools may facilitate consistent, large-scale cardiac contouring to clinically incorporate and personalize thoracic RT planning. Previous deep learning models for cardiac substructure segmentation were trained on modest sample sizes and limited substructures. Moreover, existing open-source datasets have been developed on, and for, cardiac imaging purposes, limiting generalizability to lower-quality thoracic RT simulation images and associated models. To address these limitations, we present a large, expertly annotated dataset for cardiac substructure segmentation in thoracic radiotherapy, comprising contrast-enhanced CT simulation scans from 69 patients with left-sided lung cancer and retrospective manual annotations of 21 cardiac substructures. We propose DINO-CardiacSeg, a novel cardiac substructure segmentation framework that builds on the DINOv3 vision foundation model [15]. Although DINOv3 has demonstrated strong performance in natural-image and medical-image segmentation tasks, its direct application to CT is challenged by substantial domain gaps, including imaging intensity characteristics and the need to encode anatomically meaningful information. To address these limitations, we developed a domain-adapted foundation model workflow that uses large-scale selfsupervised learning to learn CT-specific representations. We curated a large, diverse CT dataset from four public sources and performed domain-adaptive pretraining to tailor DINOv3 foundation model to the CT imaging domain. Building on this pretrained model, we introduced a novel segmentation architecture designed for high-resolution cardiac
substructure delineation. The proposed model incorporates (1) a DINOv3 adapter module that injects domain-adapted multi-scale features via deformable attention and (2) a hierarchical deformable attention decoder optimized to capture fine-grained anatomic boundaries while preserving global cardiac context. DINO-CardiacSeg outperformed multiple state-of-the-art 2D segmentation methods (nnU-Net [16], U-Net++ [17], Swin-UNET [18], and Dino-UNet [19]) on our institutional cardiac substructure dataset and, after fine-tuning, maintained superior segmentation accuracy on the public TotalSegmentator cardiac subset [20]. By enabling precise substructure-level dose assessment, this approach supports patientspecific radiotherapy planning and advances precision cardiac dosimetry to improve the prediction and prevention of radiation-induced cardiotoxicity.
Materials & methods
Dataset characteristics
Sixty-nine patients with left-sided lung cancer (36 male, 33 female) treated at a single institution with photon radiation therapy (RT) between 2015 and 2025 were identified from institutional electronic health records (Supplementary Materials Table A.1). The cohort represents patients diagnosed with both non-small cell and small cell lung cancers. Eligibility criteria included the availability of a contrastenhanced CT simulation scan within 1 month prior to treatment start. All patients received the contrast agent Omnipaque 350 intravenously prior to acquisition of simulation images. Images were acquired using Siemens CT scanners with in-plane resolution of 0.71–1.36 mm and slice thickness of 2–5 mm, tube voltage of 100–120 kVp, and tube current ranging from 91–645 mA. Patient-protected health information (PHI) metadata was removed from the DICOM files prior to model training and testing.
Substructure annotations
For each patient, 21 cardiac substructures were manually delineated on a contrast-enhanced CT scan by a trained annotator. These included the heart chambers (right and left atria, right and left ventricles), valves (mitral, tricuspid, aortic, and pulmonary), conduction nodes (sinoatrial and atrioventricular), major vessels and related structures (aortic arch, ascending and descending aorta, superior and inferior vena cava, pulmonary artery and veins), and four coronary arteries (right and left coronary arteries, left anterior descending, and left circumflex). All segmentations were performed in Velocity 4.1 (Varian Medical Systems). To promote institutional consistency and minimize atlas-related variability, contours were generated through an integrated approach combining multiple gold-standard references. The RTOG “Atlases for Organs at Risk (OARs) in Thoracic Radiation Therapy” and Feng et al. atlas served as primary visual guides for the chambers, great vessels, and coronary arteries [21,22]. Heart valves were contoured following the Socha et al. atlas, while delineation of the conduction nodes was guided by Loap et al. [23,24]. The cranial and caudal boundaries of each substructure were further informed by Vaugier et al. and Milo et al. [25,26]. A consolidated institutional framework was then developed to harmonize these references and account for patient-specific anatomical variability. Comprehensive written contouring guidelines for all 21 substructures are available upon request. All contours were reviewed by a board-certified radiation oncologist and two board-certified medical physicists to ensure clinical accuracy.
Domain-adaptive self-supervised pretraining
