odel to recover the semantics of masked patches. A masked input image was passed through the student network, and its prototype scores were compared with the teacher’s scores at the corresponding unmasked locations. Additionally, a prototype regularization loss LKoleo was applied to promote a uniform distribution of feature prototypes.
L Stage1 = L DINO + L iBOT + 0.1⋅L Koleo.
In stage 2, we adapted the SSL framework for higher-resolution training, motivated by the relatively small volume and fine anatomical detail of cardiac substructures such as the coronary arteries. To better preserve these structures, we increased the input image resolution to 512 × 512 to continue pretraining. However, simple upscaling to higher resolution may degrade local feature quality, as global and local SSL objectives are only weakly correlated. To mitigate this limitation, we incorporated Gram anchoring. This approach regularizes the Gram matrix, which is the matrix of all pairwise dot products of patch features within an image. Specifically, we encouraged the Gram matrix of the student network to remain aligned with that of an earlier teacher checkpoint, referred to as the Gram teacher, which retained stronger dense (local) features from early training. Formally, given an image with P patches and a network that operates in dimension d, let XS and XG denote the P × d matrix of L2-normalized local features of the student and the Gram teacher, respectively. We defined the loss L Gram as follows:
