multi-scale feature maps at 1/8, 1/16, and 1/32 of the input resolution. The transformer encoder layers of ViT were divided evenly into four blocks (each containing three layers). For each block, we introduced two cross-attention interactions between the ViT features and the convolutional features. In the first crossattention layer, the flattened CNN feature maps were used as keys and values, while the ViT block output served as queries. In the second cross-attention layer, the queries came from the updated features of the previous layer, and the keys and values came from the ViT block output. These bidirectional interactions allowed the adapter to fuse global ViT context with local convolutional detail, producing high-quality multi-scale representations. After crossattention fusion, the resulting features were reshaped into spatial maps at 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, and 1/32 resolution. (3) (3). Multi-scale deformable attention decoder. With the feature pyramids, the decoder leveraged deformable attention to aggregate cross-scale context. It first projected each feature map to a common channel dimension using 1 × 1 convolutions followed by group normalization. Next, the features were passed to a multi-scale deformable transformer encoder, which aggregated information across spatial locations and feature levels using deformable attention. Lastly, the feature maps were progressively upsampled via lateral 1 × 1 convolutions and 3 × 3 convolutions to obtain high-resolution predictions for cardiac substructures.
Implementation
Both pretraining and finetuning were performed on a NVIDIA DGX A100 cluster. Pretraining used two A100 GPUs (80G) for approximately two days. We pretrained for a total of 120k iterations, using a global batch size of 512. For stage 1, we used a learning rate of 2e-4 and trained for 100k iterations. For stage 2, an exponential moving average (EMA) model at 20k iterations was selected as the Gram teacher, and pretraining was continued using the gram anchoring objective for an additional 20k iterations. Finetuning experiments were performed within the nnU-Net framework [16] in PyTorch. All models were trained for 1,000 epochs, with each epoch consisting of 250 training steps. For the CardiacSubstructSeg dataset, CT images were resampled to an inplane resolution of 0.97 mm while retaining the original axial spacing, and a patch size of 512 × 512 was used. For the TotalSegmentator cardiac dataset, images were resampled to an in-plane resolution of 1.5 mm with a patch size of 256 × 256.
Baseline comparisons and evaluation metrics
We performed extensive comparisons between DINO-CardiacSeg and several state-of-the-art deep learning segmentation methods commonly used in medical imaging. The evaluated baseline models included: (1) nnU-Net [16], (2) U-Net++ [17], (3) Swin-UNET [18], and (4) DINOUNet [19]. All models were trained and evaluated using five-fold cross-validation on the CardiacSubstructSeg dataset. For each fold, patient-level splits were used to ensure that scans from the same patient do not appear in both training and testing sets. Model performance was evaluated across all 21 annotated cardiac substructures, and metrics were computed on a per-structure basis and then averaged across structures and patients. Segmentation accuracy was assessed using four standard metrics: Dice similarity coefficient (DSC), 95th percentile Hausdorff distance (HD95), surface dice coefficient (SDC), and average symmetric surface distance (ASSD). DSC and SDC quantify volumetric and surface overlap, respectively, while HD95 and ASSD evaluate boundary accuracy and robustness to outliers. Surface dice coefficient was computed on the predicted and reference segmentation surfaces in physical space. Specifically, let Sp and Sg denote the predicted and ground-truth surfaces, respectively, and let d(x, S) denote the minimum Euclidean distance from surface point x to surface S. SDC at tolerance τ was defined as
