istance metrics (HD95, ASSD) were assigned a finite penalty equal to the maximum image diagonal of the scan, computed as the Euclidean distance across the full image volume. These failure-penalized values were used consistently for all patient-level summary statistics, hypothesis tests, and any main-text tables reporting these metrics. Per-substructure failure rates are reported separately in Supplementary Materials Table A.3 to provide a descriptive breakdown of failure frequency by method and structure. This protocol ensures that all patients contribute to summary statistics and statistical tests regardless of prediction quality, avoiding selection bias from case exclusion.
Results
As summarized in Table A.2 of the Supplementary Materials, DINOCardiacSeg achieved the highest overall performance across all evaluation metrics on the CardiacSubstructSeg dataset. In the prespecified overall internal analysis, after Bonferroni correction for four overall metrics (α = 0.0125), DINO-CardiacSeg demonstrated statistically significant improvement compared with nnU-Net in DSC (p = 0.001), SDC (p = 0.0003), and ASSD (p = 0.008). Although DINO-CardiacSeg also achieved a lower HD95 (p = 0.024), this difference did not meet the corrected significance threshold for the four-metric family. In contrast, transformer-based models without domain-adaptive pretraining (SwinUNET and DINO-UNet) exhibited substantially inferior performance, particularly in surface-based metrics, suggesting limited robustness for fine-grained cardiac anatomy in thoracic CT images. Organ-level analysis further highlighted the strengths of DINOCardiacSeg across both large and small cardiac structures (Table 1). For this separate organ-group family of tests, Bonferroni correction was applied across 10 comparisons (α = 0.005). For cardiac chambers and
