Discussion
In this study, we introduce DINO-CardiacSeg, a CT-domain-adapted foundation model framework for the delineation of 21 cardiac substructures on contrast-enhanced RT simulation CT. By leveraging largescale self-supervised pretraining on 13,799 CT volumes (4,440,784 axial slices) aggregated from four public datasets, the proposed approach addresses the domain mismatch between natural-image pretrained DINOv3 and clinical CT imaging. The incorporation of a DINOv3 adapter with deformable attention enables effective integration of CTspecific multi-scale representations, while the hierarchical deformable attention decoder facilitates precise delineation of small, low-contrast cardiac substructures while preserving global anatomic context. Additionally, we curated a detailed cardiac substructure segmentation dataset tailored to thoracic radiotherapy. Compared with prior studies
that are characterized by smaller cohort sizes and narrowly defined cardiac anatomy, this study introduces a larger and more anatomically comprehensive dataset derived from contrast-enhanced lung radiotherapy simulation CTs (Table 4). We benchmarked DINO-CardiacSeg and multiple state-of-the-art 2D segmentation models (nnU-Net [16], U-Net++ [17], Swin-UNET [18], and Dino-UNet [19]) using our cardiac substructure segmentation dataset aforementioned. Across all substructures, the proposed model achieved superior segmentation accuracy, demonstrating robust improvements over existing state-of-the-art methods. To further assess transferability, we evaluated fine-tuning performance on the publicly available TotalSegmentator cardiac subset [20]. DINO-CardiacSeg maintained the highest segmentation performance, indicating that CT domain-adapted pretraining facilitates effective adaptation after finetuning to a dataset with differing annotation protocols and imaging
