characteristics. In conclusion, DINO-CardiacSeg achieved the best overall performance on internal cross-validation and maintained superior accuracy in a transfer-learning evaluation on a distinct public dataset. These findings support the utility of CT domain-adapted pretraining for adaptation to new cardiac segmentation tasks, but do not constitute direct external validation without target-dataset adaptation. By enabling scalable and consistent substructure-level cardiac contouring, this work supports precision cardiac dosimetry and provides a practical foundation for improving prediction and prevention of radiation-associated cardiotoxicity in patients receiving thoracic radiotherapy. Although our study provides important new insights and a further refined technologic approach, several limitations highlight areas for future work. First, the CardiacSubstructSeg cohort size remains modest (n=69) and originates from a single healthcare system, and acquisition was limited to contrast-enhanced simulation CT. Performance on noncontrast simulation CT and across multi-institutional scanners and protocols requires further validation. Second, substructures such as conduction nodes are not directly visible on CT and thus rely on atlasguided inference; while this reflects clinical contouring practice, it introduces uncertainty in the “ground truth” and may cap achievable performance. Third, we evaluated segmentation quality using geometric metrics but did not directly quantify downstream clinical impact (e.g., changes in substructure dose metrics, plan quality, or toxicity prediction). While only objective assessments were included in this analysis, subsequent studies will include expert-completed subjective evaluations of the model-generated cardiac substructure OAR contours to assess their suitability for clinical treatment planning. Finally, our approach is currently slice-based; extending to 3D or 2.5D contexts may further improve continuity and reduce occasional discontinuities across slices for thin structures. Our future work will investigate multi-institutional external validation on both contrast and non-contrast simulation CT, including patients treated with different techniques and motion-management strategies. Clinically, integrating DINO-CardiacSeg into a planning pipeline and evaluating effects on substructure dose–response modeling would provide direct evidence of utility. Methodologically, exploring uncertainty estimation and automatic quality assurance could improve safety for clinical deployment, particularly for small structures where occasional failure modes can disproportionately affect dosimetry. Finally, expanding annotations to additional substructures and standardizing contouring definitions across centers may facilitate harmonized studies and broader adoption.
